In one aspect, a computer-implemented method for detecting usage of one or more devices of interest from utility meter consumption data is provided, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, time-series consumption data generated by a utility meter associated with a premises;

determining, from the time-series consumption data, a plurality of positive consumption variations corresponding to increases in measured consumption and a plurality of negative consumption variations corresponding to decreases in measured consumption;

identifying a plurality of candidate usage events by matching respective positive consumption variations with respective negative consumption variations based at least in part on one or more of magnitude similarity, temporal ordering, duration, or a consumption-change threshold;

forming one or more event blocks by grouping candidate usage events that are temporally proximate or that satisfy a predetermined relationship indicative of related operation;

extracting, for each event block, one or more features including at least one of an aggregate consumption value, a duration, a variation magnitude, a start time, an end time, a recurrence characteristic, or a shape characteristic of the time-series consumption data;

clustering the one or more event blocks based on the extracted features to generate one or more clusters representative of appliance usage patterns;

comparing at least one cluster or at least one event block with reference information associated with the one or more devices of interest; and

determining, based on the comparison, whether the one or more devices of interest were used during one or more time intervals represented in the time-series consumption data.

1. A computer-implemented method comprising:

receiving a time series of electrical consumption measurements from a smart meter associated with a premises;

analyzing the time series to detect state-transition events corresponding to changes in electrical load, the state-transition events comprising at least one of on-events and off-events;

associating detected state-transition events with one another to form appliance usage blocks, each appliance usage block representing a period of operation of an electrical load;

extracting one or more features from the appliance usage blocks;

classifying the appliance usage blocks, based at least in part on the extracted features, into one or more predefined device clusters corresponding to respective categories of electrical devices; and

inferring, based on the classified appliance usage blocks, whether one or more target devices were used or not used at the premises during a time interval.

1. **A computer-implemented method comprising:**

   receiving, by one or more processors, a time series of energy consumption readings from one or more utility meters associated with a property;

   detecting, from the time series, a plurality of energy-change events, each energy-change event corresponding to an increase or decrease in measured energy consumption;

   identifying, among the plurality of energy-change events, one or more on-events associated with switching on of one or more electrical devices and one or more off-events associated with switching off of the one or more electrical devices;

   pairing at least one on-event with at least one corresponding off-event based at least in part on a magnitude of the increase and a magnitude of the decrease;

   generating, from the paired on-event and off-event, a usage occurrence representing an operating interval of a device;

   grouping a plurality of usage occurrences into one or more usage groups based on similarity of one or more operating characteristics;

   classifying at least one usage group into an appliance cluster associated with an appliance type; and

   determining, based on the appliance cluster, usage of a specific appliance at the property.

2. **The method of claim 1,** wherein the energy consumption readings comprise electrical power readings sampled at regular or irregular time intervals.

3. **The method of claim 1,** wherein detecting the plurality of energy-change events comprises identifying step changes in the time series exceeding a threshold magnitude.

4. **The method of claim 1,** wherein pairing the at least one on-event with the at least one corresponding off-event comprises comparing power-change magnitude, duration between events, time of day, or combinations thereof.

5. **The method of claim 1,** wherein each usage occurrence comprises at least one of a start time, an end time, a duration, an average power level, a peak power level, or an energy consumed during the operating interval.

6. **The method of claim 1,** wherein grouping the plurality of usage occurrences comprises clustering the usage occurrences based on power draw, duration, frequency of use, temporal pattern, or energy consumption.

7. **The method of claim 1,** wherein classifying the at least one usage group comprises comparing characteristics of the usage group with stored appliance signatures.

8. **The method of claim 1,** wherein the appliance cluster corresponds to at least one of a refrigerator, heating appliance, cooling appliance, water heater, lighting load, laundry appliance, cooking appliance, pool pump, or electric vehicle charger.

9. **The method of claim 1,** further comprising outputting an appliance-specific usage report indicating an estimated energy consumption of the specific appliance over a selected time period.

10. **A system comprising:**

   one or more processors; and

   one or more non-transitory computer-readable storage media storing instructions that, when executed by the one or more processors, cause the system to:

   receive energy consumption readings from one or more meters at a property;

   detect increases and decreases in energy consumption corresponding to device switching events;

   pair corresponding increases and decreases to define device operating intervals;

   group the device operating intervals into usage occurrences having similar characteristics;

   classify the usage occurrences into appliance clusters; and

   determine usage of one or more specific appliances at the property based on the appliance clusters.

11. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause the one or more processors to perform operations comprising:**

   receiving meter-based energy consumption data for a property;

   detecting positive and negative changes in the energy consumption data;

   matching positive changes with corresponding negative changes to identify respective appliance run cycles;

   aggregating appliance run cycles into usage groups;

   assigning the usage groups to appliance-type clusters; and

   estimating usage of specific appliances corresponding to the appliance-type clusters.

1. A computer-implemented method for monitoring utility meter data to determine whether one or more devices of interest in a home have been used, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, time-series utility consumption data generated by a utility meter associated with the home;

detecting, by the one or more processors, variations in the time-series utility consumption data relative to a baseline or prior consumption level;

identifying, by the one or more processors, events in the time-series utility consumption data based at least in part on the detected variations, each event corresponding to a change in utility consumption over a time interval;

grouping, by the one or more processors, temporally related events into one or more blocks, each block representing a candidate operational episode of a utility-consuming device;

extracting, by the one or more processors, one or more features from each block, the one or more features including at least one of magnitude, duration, shape, timing, frequency, or aggregate consumption associated with the block;

classifying, by the one or more processors, each block into one of a plurality of clusters based on the extracted features, wherein at least one cluster is associated with operation of a device of interest in the home; and

determining, by the one or more processors, based on the classified blocks, whether the device of interest has been used during a monitored period or whether usage of the device of interest is absent during the monitored period.
